.\" Manual page for makedog.
.TH MAKEDOG 1 2026-07-23 "makedog" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
makedog \- a runner for server binaries: restart on builds, log every
run, send signals, run make targets, and more
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B makedog
[\fBwatch\fR] [\fB\-c\fR \fIconfig\fR] \fIbinary-path\fR
.br
.B makedog
\fIrun#\fR|\fBlatest\fR[\fB~\fIN\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.BR runs | current | next | tail
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B search
\fIregex\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.BR info | path
[\fIref\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B diff
[\fIa\fR [\fIb\fR]] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.BR crash | spin
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.BR status | restart | stop | start | quit
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B signal
\fIname\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B loglevel
[\fIlevel\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B makedog
.B help
[\fIverb\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B makedog
supervises a server binary during development. It starts the binary,
watches the compiled file for changes, and restarts the process when a
new build lands. It deliberately does not watch source code or drive
recompilation; that is left to your editor, agent, or build system.
.PP
What it supervises is live everywhere: other terminals and agents can
watch in real time, without holding the process themselves.
.B tail
follows output across restarts,
.B current
gates on the run of the build now on disk,
.B next
catches a coming run from its first line, and
.B \-\-follow
streams one already in flight.
.PP
A \fIrun\fR is one execution of your binary, from start to
exit. And many runs are grouped into a \fIlineage\fR.
.PP
Your \fIproject\fR is your working directory. We anchor from here to
find binaries, read configs, and match runs & logs. A project can
have many binaries, each with its own lineage. A binary inside
the project is identified by its relative path, so \fIbin/server\fR,
\fI./bin/server\fR, or its full path, name one lineage.
.PP
Discovery favors convenience over exactness. Generally, an
unspecified run means the most recent, and an unspecified binary means the
most recently active one. Also, a renamed or moved binary starts a
fresh lineage rather than erroring.
.PP
A bare path argument implies
.BR watch .
A bare run reference implies
.BR show
/
.BR latest.
For example, paths include slashes, like \fI./path\fR or \fIbin/name\fR.
Run references look like \fB33\fR, or
.BR latest [\fB~\fIN\fR].
.PP
Every run is recorded to a durable per-project store as JSONL, one
record per output line plus lifecycle events, and can be replayed,
tailed, searched, and diffed later. Older logs are compressed in the
background, and pruned per the retention policy (see
.B CONFIGURATION
below).
.PP
A running instance listens on a unix socket, so a second terminal can
query status, restart or stop the binary, or deliver signals remotely.
.PP
Each project can customize the signal menu. Taper the list and name
each signal (e.g. "reload config"). See
.B CONFIGURATION
below.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.BI watch " binary-path"
Supervise a binary (or script). Restarts it when the file's
modification time changes, logs the run, and accepts interactive
keypresses (see
.B INTERACTIVE KEYS
below).
.TP
\fIrun#\fR, \fBlatest\fR[\fB~\fIN\fR]
Replay a recorded run (longhand:
.B show
\fIref\fR).
.B latest~1
is the run before the latest, and so on.
.TP
.B runs
List this directory's recorded runs;
.B \-\-long
prints full metadata cards.
.TP
.B current
Show the run of the current binary on disk, possibly waiting for it to begin.
Contrast
.BR latest ,
the most recent (or live) run whatever binary produced it, and
.BR next ,
whatever run starts next.
Runs are matched by the binary's
hash, so
.B current
may be called at any point after a build.
.TP
.B next
Wait for the next run to begin and stream it from its start.
.TP
.B tail
Follow live output across restarts, endlessly.
.TP
.BI search " regex"
Grep recorded runs (alias:
.BR grep );
exits 1 when nothing matches.
.TP
.BR info " [\fIref\fR]"
One run's metadata card, default latest;
.B \-\-json
emits one object.
.TP
.BR path " [\fIref\fR]"
Print a run log's file path; with no ref, the lineage directory.
.TP
.BR diff " [\fIa\fR [\fIb\fR]]"
Unified diff of two runs' output, default latest~1 vs latest.
.TP
.B crash
The runs that tripped the spin stop, back to back, oldest first, each
under a header led by its exit status (alias: spin). Exit 1 when no spin
was found and the last runs were shown instead.
.TP
.B status
List live makedog instances for the project.
.TP
.BR restart ", " stop ", " start
Command a live instance's binary remotely.
.TP
.BI signal " name"
Send the supervised binary a signal remotely, e.g. HUP or USR1.
.TP
.BI loglevel " level"
Set the supervised binary's log level, by whatever transport the
project's
.B [loglevel]
configuration declares. With no \fIlevel\fR, or with
.BR \-\-list ,
list the configured levels instead. Every application is ephemeral: an
env level spawns one run at that level and requires a restart
acknowledgment (a prompt, or
.BR \-\-restart ),
a signal or command level pokes the live run, and the next restart
reverts to baseline either way. Permanent levels belong in the
project's own configuration.
.TP
.BR help " [\fIverb\fR]"
The overview, or one verb's page: its options, exit status, and examples.
.B makedog
\fIverb\fR
.B \-\-help
is the same page.
.SH OPTIONS
Each verb takes the options listed under it and no others;
.B makedog
\fIverb\fR
.B \-\-help
prints the same list. The common options are described once, at the end.
.SS watch
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIpath\fR"
Path to a configuration file, rather than searching for
.BR .makedog.toml .
.SS show (a run reference), current
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Keep streaming until the run exits.
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Only the last \fIcount\fR records.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " age"
Only records within this age, like 30s or 5m.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " regex"
Stream until a line matches (implies
.BR \-\-follow ):
exit 0 if matched, 1 if the run ended first.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Give up after this duration, like 30s, exit 2.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-plain ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS next
.TP
.BI \-\-until " regex"
Stream until a line matches: exit 0 if matched, 1 if the run ended first.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Give up after this duration, like 30s, exit 2.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-plain ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS tail
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Initial backlog from the current run, default 10; successors stream
whole from their first line.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " regex"
Stream until a line matches, exit 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Give up after this duration, like 30s, exit 2.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-plain ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS runs
.TP
.B \-\-long
Full metadata cards, as
.B info
shows.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS search
.TP
.BI \-\-runs " a..b"
Limit to a run number or range, like 130..135.
.TP
.BI \-\-since " age"
Only runs active within this age, like 2d or 6h.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-binaries
Search every binary recorded for the project.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-plain ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS info
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS path
.BR \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS diff
.BR \-\-plain ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS crash
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Only the last N records of each run.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-plain ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS status
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS restart, stop, start, quit, signal
.TP
.BI \-\-instance " pid"
Target this makedog pid. Required whenever more than one instance is
live;
.B status
lists them.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS loglevel
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the levels the
.B [loglevel]
configuration defines, or the generic setting. Bare
.B loglevel
does the same.
.TP
.B \-\-restart
Permit the restart an env-applied level requires, without asking.
.TP
.BI \-\-instance " pid"
Target this makedog pid, when several are live.
.PP
Also
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-binary ", " \-C .
.SS Common options
.TP
.BR \-\-json ", " \-\-jsonl
Machine-readable output: raw JSONL records from the readers, one
object per run or instance from the listers, the card or response as
one object elsewhere.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Strip ANSI: the binary's own escapes and makedog's colors. In
.BR search ,
hits only; in
.BR diff ,
no color on change lines.
.TP
.BI \-\-binary " path"
Which binary's runs, when several are recorded for the project.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
Operate on another project directory.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
The verb's page: options, exit status, and examples.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
While watching, makedog reads single keypresses from the terminal:
.TP
.B c
Clear the screen.
.TP
.B h
Show key help.
.TP
.B k
Write a mark into the run log.
.TP
.B l
Choose a named log level from a menu and apply it (see
.B [loglevel]
under
.B CONFIGURATION
below). An env level restarts into the level without further ado, we
assume
.BR \-\-restart
consent.
.TP
.B m
Run make.
.TP
.B q
Quit makedog.
.TP
.B r
Restart the binary.
.TP
.B s
Choose a signal from a menu and send it to the binary.
.TP
.B t
Choose a make target from a menu and run it.
.TP
.B x
Start or stop the binary.
.SH CONFIGURATION
On
.BR watch ,
makedog loads
.B .makedog.toml
from the current directory (or the file named by
.BR \-\-config ).
A missing file is fine; a malformed one is ignored with a warning.
.SS [[signals]]
Configured signals replace the default
.B s
menu, which then offers only these, in config order. The remote
.B signal
verb is unaffected:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[[signals]]
signal = "USR1"
name   = "rotate logs"   # optional display name
.fi
.RE
.SS [loglevel]
Describes how the
.B loglevel
verb works, given a level argument. Makedog defines no levels of
its own. You can either use one of the bare words
.B env
(a variable name) or
.B command
(a shell command with %s), and these will pass your provided level as-is, without checks:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[loglevel]
env          = "DEBUG"          # restart with env DEBUG=<level>
                                #   --or--
command      = "curl ..%s.."    # run command, replacing %s with <level>
.fi
.RE
.PP
Or you can define all the valid levels, each with the same mechanism, or mix in different ones.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[loglevel]
env          = "DEBUG"
debug.env    = "app:*,http:*"   # restart with env DEBUG=app:*,http:*
info.env     = "app:*"          # restart with env DEBUG=app:*
up.signal    = "USR1"           # send the process USR1
mute.command = "curl -sX PUT localhost:8080/log/level -d off" # no %s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each named entry sets exactly one of
.BR env ", " signal ", or " command .
An env level restarts the binary with the assignment in its
environment, for that run alone; a signal or command level acts on the
live process without restarting it. Note that the prior bare
.B env
now defines the environment variable name for each value.
.SS [logs]
Tunes run log retention. A watch-mode makedog runs one maintenance pass
shortly after startup, compressing idle runs with zstd and pruning by
count, age, and total size. The newest two runs are never touched.
Setting a field to zero disables that rule.
.TP
.B compress_after
Compress runs idle longer than this (default "24h").
.TP
.B keep_runs
Keep at most this many runs (default 500).
.TP
.B max_age
Delete runs idle longer than this, e.g. "90d" (default: never).
.TP
.B max_total
Delete oldest runs while the lineage exceeds this size, e.g. "512MB"
(default: unlimited).
.SH STOP SEMANTICS
However a stop arrives \(em a rebuild restart, the
.B r
or
.B x
keys, a remote
.BR stop " or " restart ,
or makedog itself exiting \(em the binary first receives SIGTERM and
two seconds to exit gracefully. An unresponsive process is then dealt
SIGKILL, noted in the log. Output produced during shutdown is drained
into the run log before it is sealed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .makedog.toml
Per-project configuration, read from the working directory.
.TP
.B ~/.local/state/makedog
The run store. Each (working directory, binary path) pair owns a
lineage of numbered runs. Overridden by
.BR MAKEDOG_STATE_DIR .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MAKEDOG_STATE_DIR
Base directory of the run store, in place of ~/.local/state/makedog.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Honored when
.B MAKEDOG_STATE_DIR
is unset: the store lives in $XDG_STATE_HOME/makedog.
.TP
.B PAGER
Pager for replayed runs when stdout is a terminal (default "less
\-FRX").
.SH EXIT STATUS
The readers exit 0 when the run was shown or
.B \-\-until
matched, 1 when the run ended before
.B \-\-until
matched, and 2 on
.B \-\-timeout
expiry or when no run turns up.
.B search
exits 0 on a match, 1 when nothing matches.
.B diff
exits 0 when the runs' output is identical, 1 when it differs, and 2 on
trouble, like a bad reference. Other verbs exit 0 on success and
nonzero on error; a flag error exits 2.
.SH EXAMPLES
Supervise a server and restart it whenever it is rebuilt:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog bin/server
.fi
.RE
.PP
Replay the previous run, plainly, through the pager:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog latest~1 \-\-plain
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rebuild, then block until the new build reports readiness, or give up
after 30 seconds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make && makedog current \-\-until 'listening on' \-\-timeout 30s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search the last two days of runs for errors:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog search 'ERROR|panic' \-\-since 2d
.fi
.RE
.PP
Tell the running server to reload its config, from another terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog signal HUP
.fi
.RE
.PP
Bounce the server into debug logging for one run, then see what the
extra verbosity caught:
.PP
.RS
.nf
makedog loglevel debug \-\-restart
makedog diff
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR make (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR less (1)
